@@ -7,15 +7,19 @@ no ranking logic.
 ## What it does
 - Fetches Client-backend gateway routes (`/recommendations`, `/videos/similar`, `/api/v1/search/videos`, `/api/video`, `/api/channels`, `GET` and `POST /api/translate`). The video page also reads the source PeerTube instance's API directly: the video metadata fallback, `/api/v1/config`, channels, similar-video view counts and comments. Caption tracks are never read from the instance by the browser; they arrive only through `/api/translate`.
 - Renders the home feed, the feed of videos similar to one video (`?id=`), and the video page.
-- The home feed has five modes: Recommendations, Trending, Recent, Random and Popular. A switcher of `[data-feed-mode]` buttons in a `role="group"` picks one, and the active button carries `aria-pressed="true"`. For what each mode orders by, see `engine/server/api/recommendations/docs/OVERVIEW.md`. Trending, Recent and Popular are shown in the Engine's order; only Random is shuffled.
+- The home feed has six modes: Recommendations, Trending, Recent, Random, Popular and Following. A switcher of `[data-feed-mode]` buttons in a `role="group"` picks one, and the active button carries `aria-pressed="true"`. For what each mode orders by, see `engine/server/api/recommendations/docs/OVERVIEW.md`. Trending, Recent, Popular and Following are shown in the Engine's order; only Random is shuffled. Following needs a profile key: without one the feed shows "Following needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button." and sends no request. An empty Following feed reads "No videos found.".
 - The mode in effect comes from the URL's `?mode=`, then the legacy `?random=` (any value other than `0` means Random), then the JSON object `{mode}` stored in `localStorage` under `feedParams:v1`, then Recommendations. An unknown value in the URL or in storage reads as Recommendations, except `hot`, which reads as Trending (`parseFeedMode`), so a `?mode=hot` link or a stored `hot` opens Trending instead of sending a mode the Engine answers with 400. Choosing a mode stores it, sets `?mode=` and reloads the feed from the first page. `?id=` views ignore the mode. "Back to recommended" links to `/?mode=recommendations`, so it does not land on the stored mode.
 - `src/data/feed-params.ts` is the one module that reads the feed parameters and the NSFW setting from the URL and storage, persists them, and turns them into the Engine request's query (the feed parameters through `buildSimilarUrl` in `src/data/videos.ts`, the NSFW setting through `nsfwQuery`). Page code does not build feed or NSFW query parameters itself.
 - The NSFW setting hides NSFW-flagged videos from every video list and is on by default. It is stored in `localStorage` under `nsfwFilter:v1` as `on` or `off`, apart from `feedParams:v1`, and does not follow a profile key to another browser. Only a stored `off` turns the filter off; a missing, corrupt or unreadable value means on, and when the write fails the choice still holds for the current page. Its only control is the "Hide NSFW videos" checkbox (`label.profile-nsfw`) in the home page's Profile modal, shown in the keyless, key-holding and just-issued-key states with no key needed; the video and search pages have no control of their own. Changing it reloads the home feed, and an older load that resolves after the newer one is discarded. Home feed requests in every mode, up-next (the home `?id=` view and the video page, through `buildSimilarUrl`) and search (`fetchSearchResults` in `src/data/search.ts`) send `nsfw=1` only while the filter is off, reading the setting each time a request is built. The Engine does the filtering; for what `nsfw=1` includes see `engine/server/README.md`.
-- The feed page loads another batch when scrolling reaches the end of the rows fetched so far. Each batch excludes the 500 most recently shown videos, rows already shown are dropped, and paging ends for that page view after a batch that adds nothing or fails (`createFeedPager` in `src/data/videos.ts`). A reload starts over. In Trending, Recent and Popular the Engine continues its order after the excluded rows, so paging ends after about 500 shown rows, or sooner for a visitor with a profile key whose blocked or disliked rows are removed by the Client backend (see `client/README.md`).
+- The feed page loads another batch when scrolling reaches the end of the rows fetched so far. Each batch excludes the 500 most recently shown videos, rows already shown are dropped, and paging ends for that page view after a batch that adds nothing or fails (`createFeedPager` in `src/data/videos.ts`). A reload starts over. In Trending, Recent and Popular the Engine continues its order after the excluded rows, so paging ends after about 500 shown rows, or sooner for a visitor with a profile key whose blocked or disliked rows are removed by the Client backend (see `client/README.md`). Following pages by cursor instead (`createCursorPager`): each request sends the last cursor and no exclude, and a short or empty page that carries a cursor is not the end. One batch keeps fetching through empty pages, pausing 3 s after every 4 requests to stay under the Client's rate limit, until a page has rows or no cursor comes back; only a page with no cursor ends the feed.
 - Without a profile key, keeps likes in the browser (`localLikes:v1`), written only after the Client backend accepted them. With a key, likes and dislikes live in the profile: on load each page moves any local likes into it (`POST /api/profile/likes/import`) and clears them once imported, and feed requests carry no likes.
 - Shows the visitor's reaction: the video page's Like and Dislike buttons (read from `GET /api/profile/reaction` with a key), and a mark on feed, search and similar-video cards (the Client's `reaction` row field with a key, `localLikes:v1` without). The reaction data lives in `src/data/reactions.ts`.
-- Home feed and search cards that have a video key carry Like, Dislike, Block channel and Block account buttons. Like and Dislike carry `aria-pressed`. Like works without a profile key and toggles between `like` and `undo_like`. Dislike and both Block buttons need a key. Without one they send no request and write "Disliking needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button." or "Blocking needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button." into the card's status line. A Block also dislikes the video. If that dislike fails, the card reads "Blocked <source>, but the dislike failed: <message>" and no cards are removed. Otherwise every loaded card from the same channel (`instance_domain` + `channel_id`) or account (`account_url`) is removed. A failed action, including a rejected profile key, shows its message in that card's status line. The button is disabled while the request runs and is re-enabled afterwards.
+- Home feed and search cards that have a video key carry Like, Dislike, Block channel, Block account, Follow channel and Follow account buttons. Like and Dislike carry `aria-pressed`. Like works without a profile key and toggles between `like` and `undo_like`. Dislike, both Block buttons and both Follow buttons need a key. Without one they send no request and write "Disliking needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button.", "Blocking needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button." or "Following needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button." into the card's status line. A Block also dislikes the video. If that dislike fails, the card reads "Blocked <source>, but the dislike failed: <message>" and no cards are removed. Otherwise every loaded card from the same channel (`instance_domain` + `channel_id`) or account (`account_url`) is removed. A failed action, including a rejected profile key, shows its message in that card's status line. The button is disabled while the request runs and is re-enabled afterwards.
 - On the home feed, Dislike removes the card and cannot be undone there. On search, which is not filtered by dislikes, Dislike toggles between `dislike` and `undo_dislike` and the card stays on the page with its mark, and a like replaces a dislike and the reverse. After a Block removes search cards, the next page is fetched if the end of the results is now on screen. "Showing N of M matched videos." keeps counting the rows fetched, including removed ones.
+- Follow controls (`src/data/follows.ts`) read "Follow channel"/"Unfollow channel" and "Follow account"/"Unfollow account" and toggle the follow through `/api/profile/follows` (for what the routes accept see `client/README.md`). With a key, each page loads the follow list once at start to label its controls; a failed load is only `console.warn`ed and every control reads Follow. The labels follow that one load, so after Create, Use key or Delete profile the new state shows from the next page load. Each control is disabled while its request runs, shows the result or the error, including a rejected profile key, and is re-enabled afterwards.
+  - Cards: a toggle relabels every loaded card of that channel or account in place and says `Following <label>.` or `Unfollowed this <kind>.`; it never dislikes or removes a card. A Block clears the follow label on the blocked key.
+  - Video page: `#follow-channel` and `#follow-account` sit beside the block buttons, stay disabled until the follow list is known, report in `#block-status`, and without a key say "Following needs a profile. Create one from the Profile button on the home page.". `/api/video` carries no `channelId`, so the channel button reads "Follow channel" at load even for a followed channel, until a click returns the Engine's key.
+  - Channels page: a Follow column with a per-row "Follow"/"Unfollow" button and a `.follow-status` line, channel follow only, since a row has no `account_url`.
 - Clips the video page's description to 4 lines. A "Show more"/"Show less" button (`#description-toggle`, with `aria-expanded`) appears only while the text is taller than 4 lines, and is re-checked when the width changes. The expanded state lasts for that page view only and is stored nowhere. The text is set with `textContent`.
 - Translate shows an English line over the video page's embedded player (`src/data/translate.ts`, `src/pages/video-page/translate.ts`, on the `@peertube/embed-api` and `jschannel` dependencies). The `#video-embed` src carries `api=1`, and one embed-API player is built on it per page. The `#translate-toggle` button, labelled exactly "Translate", appears only after the embed API's `ready` resolves and a profile key is held; no `/api/translate` request is made before that, and where the embed API never answers there is no toggle. The setting is stored in `localStorage` under `translate:v1`: off by default, only a stored `on` counts as on, it applies to later videos, still holds for the current page when the write fails, and does not follow a profile key to another browser. A page loaded with Translate on requests the cues exactly as a click does.
 - While Translate is on, the page reads `GET /api/translate?id=&host=` with `profileHeaders()` and `cache: "no-store"` and rejects a malformed reply; a missing `available` reads as false. `ready` cues are sorted by start then end and replace the held list. The cue containing the player's position shows in `#translate-overlay`, set with `textContent`, driven by `playbackStatusUpdate` and, after 3 s with no update, by a `getCurrentPosition()` poll every 1 s. `running` cues show the same way, and a position past the last held cue shows nothing. The overlay does not show in the embed's own fullscreen.
